<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narada Smriti</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Narada Smriti is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> legal text in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmashastra">Dharmashastra</a> tradition. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> profile, it was compiled or refined during the Gupta period alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihaspati-smriti">Brihaspati Smriti</a>. These texts codified civil and criminal law along Brahmanical lines, covering property, inheritance and royal obligations.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Nāradasmṛti belongs to the Dharmaśāstra tradition of legal maxims on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>. Dates for its composition are uncertain, and the best timeframe offered is between 100 BCE and 400 CE. One recension claims that Manu Prajapati composed a text of 100,000 verses and 1080 chapters that the sages Narada, Markandeya and Sumati Bhargava abridged to 4,000 verses, making the Nāradasmṛti the ninth chapter, on legal procedure. Lariviere judges this account a later myth. He also argues that the text had no single author but was compiled, by an individual or a group, from verses that a community attributed to the sage Narada.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Known as the juridical text par excellence, it is the only Dharmaśāstra text that does not cover righteous conduct and penance, and deals only with procedural and substantive law. It opens with a short introduction to law and the courts, then devotes a chapter to each of eighteen titles of law, which the Manusmṛti also mentions under somewhat different names. Its manner suggests that its authors addressed practitioners who applied the law to everyday cases.</p><p>Three recensions survive. The minor recension has 879 verses and the second recension had 550 originally, which Julius Jolly later enlarged to 1,028 verses by adding the minor recension's. The third, from Newari manuscripts and the Nāradīyamanusaṃhitā, has 870 verses and is seen as the oldest. They differ in content: one contains a chapter found in no other manuscript, and they list different numbers of ordeals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Rulers and their governments valued the text, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> and in southeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, probably as an aid to carrying out their duty of ruling justly. A twelfth-century inscription of Jaya Harivarman in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, says that his court officials were expert in all the dharmashastras, especially the Naradiya and Bhargaviya.</p><p>Jolly translated the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in 1876, making it available to legal scholars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for the first time, and a second in 1879. Scholars accepted it readily because its style and structure resembled Roman legal texts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> used the translation as a reference. In 1989 Lariviere produced a critical translation using all three recensions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nāradasmṛti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Narada Smriti</b> and Brihaspati Smriti legal texts, compiled or refined during this period, codified civil and criminal law along Brahmanical lines, addressing property rights, inheritance, contracts, and royal obligations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
